  "content": {
    "id": "p55-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "The polynomial $P(x)$ is divided by $(x-1)(x+2)$.\nSuppose that the quotient is $Q(x)$ and the remainder is $R(x)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p55-q9-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Explain why $R(x)$ must be in the form $ax+b$, where $a$ and $b$ are constants.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The divisor has degree 2, so the remainder has degree less than 2 and can be written as $ax+b$.",
          "worked": "The remainder must have degree less than the divisor's degree. Since $(x-1)(x+2)$ has degree 2, the remainder can be written as $R(x)=ax+b$, where $a$ and $b$ are constants.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p55-q9-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "If $P(1)=2$ and $P(-2)=5$, find $a$ and $b$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a=-1,\\ b=3$.",
          "worked": "$$P(x)=(x-1)(x+2)Q(x)+ax+b.$$\nAt the divisor's roots, $P(x)=ax+b$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(1)&=a+b=2,\\\\P(-2)&=-2a+b=5.\\end{align*}$$\nSubtracting the second equation from the first:\n$$\\begin{align*}3a&=-3,\\\\a&=-1,\\\\b&=2-(-1)=3.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 56
      }
    ]
  },
